  "articleBody": "Installing a new threshold might seem straightforward, but there are a few common slip-ups that can lead to big headaches down the road. First off, a lot of folks forget to account for the jamb-to-jamb width, ending up with a threshold that's either too short or too long. It's crucial to get those measurements spot-on! Another frequent mistake is not checking the floor-to-door clearance, which can cause the door to drag or not seal properly. \n\nThen there's the issue of over-tightening fasteners, which can warp the threshold or even damage the subfloor. Skipping sealant around the edges is a big no-no, as it’s your first line of defense against drafts and water. And seriously, never leave the threshold completely level or, worse, pitched *into* your home—it needs a slight slope away from the building to ensure water drains outward, especially for exterior doors. If you're tackling a complex install, or if you're worried about leaks and binding, it's a good idea to chat with a professional like Abraham Becerra and his team at HH Remodeling LLC in Aurora. We're happy to help ensure it's done right.",
  "datePublished": "2026-08-09"
